      "edition": "Monday 9 November",
      "sections": [
        {
          "kicker": "Post-match view",
          "heading": "Planet Rugby: All Blacks leaned on Andrew Brace's penalties in 43-21 win",
          "body": "Planet Rugby argues the 43-21 win at Murrayfield flattered New Zealand, who were 'bereft of ideas and penetration' and relied on referee Andrew Brace for points. Scrum penalties did much of the work, the piece says, once the first scrum of the game was packed in the 34th minute, and lineout-drive tries were rare. Scotland, it argues, played the better rugby for the opening 40 minutes. Ethan de Groot and Fabian Holland, it says, had stronger claims to player of the match than Quinn Tupaea."
        },
        {
          "kicker": "Around the world",
